  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe current optimization pipeline uses discrete steps and local search methods that may not fully explore the solution space. This approach introduces a continuous relaxations framework combined with randomized rounding to ensure both optimality and feasibility. By leveraging the periodic nature of the problem and dual optimization techniques, we seek to bypass local optima and find structured solutions. The representation as a periodic function with Fourier basis allows capturing repeating patterns, while the dual formulation addresses the non-convex maximum overlap objective. Hyperparameter sweeping and adaptive projection mitigate issues with numerical stability and constraint violations.\n\nMathematical specification:\nVariables: Represent h(x) as a piecewise constant function with N intervals. The dual variables are defined for the KKT conditions of the minimum overlap problem.\nConstraints: \n1. Feasibility: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 on all intervals\n2. Integral: \u2211h(x_i)\u03b4x = 1\n3. Periodicity: h(x+2) = h(x)\n\nObjective:\nMinimize over h: max_{k} \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n\nFourier Basis Representation:\nLet h(x) be approximated by a finite Fourier series with unrestricted coefficients.\nFormulate the problem as a dual optimization: minimize the maximum correlation subject to domain constraints.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Initialization:\n   - Set n_points = 512 (Farthest valid limit)\n   - dx = 2.0 / n_points\n   - Project initial function to meet integral constraint using iterative scaling\n\n2. Primary Optimization:\n   - Use a dual-gradient method implementing projected gradient steps\n   - Compute dual objective via fast Fourier transform (FFT) correlation\n   - At each iteration, project seven to ten harmonics of the dual objective\n   - Apply coordinate-wise projection to maintain feasibility\n\n3. Adaptive Resolution:\n   - Add resolution by zero-padding if convergence stalls\n   - Double the number of optimization steps proportionally to increased dimension\n   - Maintain a dynamic frequency cutoff to balance computation cost\n\n4. Hyperparameter tuning:\n   - Maintain a global list of \u03b1 parameters (dual scaling)\n   - Start with \u03b1_start = 0.95 and decay exponentially (factor 0.99)\n   - Include a temperature parameter for stochastic rounding\n   - Maximum gradient steps: min(3000, budget/2)\n\n5. Rounding and Feasibility Correction:\n   - Apply randomized rounding to the continuous solution\n   - For each extremal interval, use \u03b1-perturbed rounding\n   - Re-compute integral and cap values at [0,1] post-rounding\n\n6. Validation Process:\n   - Re-calculate the maximum correlation as a validation\n   - Compare full-problem objective and projected gradient\n   - If entropy standard deviation < 1e-3, accept solution\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Harmonic frequency selection: Start with 3.14, increase to 6.28, etc.\n- Projection strength: Maintain default between 0.8 and 0.95\n- Recovery strategy: Restart dual step if below convergence threshold\n- Temperature adaptation: Decrease when below 0.38 bound\n\nCompute budget and robustness:\n- Estimation: O(n\u00b2 log n)\n- Critical timing checkpoints: FFT transforms (800ms per transform)\n- Monitoring: Abort step loop if IO repeats exceed 75% of windows\n- Validation fallback: Return projection if any extremum value violates [0,1]\n\nValidation and return contract:\nMust ensure:\n1. 0 \u2264 h_values[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n2. sum(h_values) = n_points/2 exactly\n3. Computed C_5 matches dual projection\n4. All intermediate values are finite and properly scaled\n5. Rounding preserves at least 99.5% of dual objective\n\nImplementation:\nFully specify variable bounds, FFT normalization, and stochastic projection.\nAll transforms use real-input RFFT implementation.\nOptimization steps:\nmain_loop(steps):\n    for i in range(steps):\n        compute_fft(...)\n        update_dual_objective()\n        project_gradients()\n        recompute_adaptive_resolution()\n        round_solution()\n        validate_single_step_objective()\n\nTermination criteria:\n- Minimal dual improvement (1e-6)\n- Minimal temperature (0.001)\n- Budget exhaustion\n\nError handling:\n- NaN values: Fall back to simplest projection\n- Constraint violation: Return least-violating neighbor\n- Memory overflow: Reduce resolution",
